====================================================================================
ITEM 2 -- the coefficient-sum lemma
====================================================================================
  |p(x)| <= sum|a_k| on x in [0,1]: violations in 12000 random samples = 0
  is x in [0,1] needed?  p(x)=1+x has ||p||=2 but p(1.5)=2.5 > 2  -> YES, the hypothesis is essential.
  |p^(j)(x)| <= d! * ||p|| on [0,1]: violations = 0

  WHERE IS IT APPLIED?  ranges of the arguments in this problem:
    t   = m/n,      m in [b+r+1, n]   ->  t   in ( 0, 1 ]        OK
    1-t             (Taylor centre of Hhat_{r-1}, K_{r-1})      ->  [0, 1)   OK
    s   = a/n,      a in [0, n-b-r-1] ->  s   in [ 0, 1 )        OK
    1-s = (n-a)/n                      ->  ( 0, 1 ]              OK
    NB  s := (1-t)+h = (n-m+1)/n  is the ARGUMENT of h_{r-1}, but the Taylor
        expansions in Delta_r are centred at 1-t and evaluated there, so the
        polynomials are never evaluated outside [0,1].  s itself satisfies
        s = (n-m+1)/n <= 1 iff m >= 1, and m >= b+r+1 >= 1, so s in (0,1] too.

====================================================================================
ITEM 3a -- falling-factorial product identity  prod_{i=k+1}^m (i-j)/i = C(k,j)/C(m,j)
====================================================================================
  prod_{i=k+1}^m (i-j) = (m-j)!/(k-j)! ,  prod_{i=k+1}^m i = m!/k!
  so LHS = (m-j)! k! / ((k-j)! m!)
  and C(k,j)/C(m,j) = [k!/(j!(k-j)!)] * [j!(m-j)!/m!] = k!(m-j)!/((k-j)! m!)
  symbolic difference (gamma form), simplified: 0
  numeric: 4764 concrete (j,k,m) triples tested, mismatches = 0

